course: dictionary
lesson: 15326
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The word snip can be an action (a verb) or a thing (a noun). To choose the right meaning, read the words around it, because they are its context.
explain: «Mia took her scissors and began to snip the paper into a star.» Here snip is an action. Mia is cutting with small, quick cuts, and the scissors and paper are the clues.
explain: «The coat was only £5 in the sale. It was a real snip!» Here snip is a noun meaning a bargain. The low price is the clue: something cheap for what it is worth.
explain: Always ask: what would fit in this sentence? Then try your meaning in place of the word and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Snip means to cut something with small, quick cuts, usually with scissors.
fact: As a noun, a snip can be a small cut, the sound of scissors, or a small piece cut off.
fact: In everyday British talk, «a snip» means a bargain: something cheap for its value.
fact: «It's a snip at £5» means the price is very good for what you get.
fact: Phrases with snip include «snip off», «snip at» and «a snip at the price».
exercise: «Mia took her scissors and began to snip the string.» What does snip mean here? | *To cut with small, quick cuts | To paint with bright colours | To hide under a table | To tie in a knot
exercise: «Tom heard a snip as the barber worked.» What is a snip here? | A small piece of wood | *The sound of scissors | A bargain | A kind of bird
exercise: «The coat was only £5 in the sale. It was a real snip!» What does snip mean? | A torn page | A very long walk | A loud noise | *A bargain, something cheap for its value
test: «Dad will snip off the loose thread with his scissors.» Which word tells us what Dad does? | loose | thread | scissors | *snip
test: Which sentence uses snip to mean a small piece cut off? | «She picked up the paper and read it.» | «She picked up the scissors from the drawer.» | *«She picked up a snip of paper from the floor.»
exam: Sam says, «A snip at £2!» What does he mean? | *The price is a good bargain | The shop is closed | Sam is sad about the price | Sam is cutting a ribbon
